# Namleh Design Memory

Use this to preserve design taste and stop repeating rejected UI patterns.

## Capture When

- Steven says a screen, flow, or mockup is good.
- Steven rejects a direction or says it feels wrong.
- A UI review identifies a reusable rule.
- A client portal, Ops Platform screen, or game menu establishes a pattern that
  future work should reuse.

## Baselines

- Ops and SaaS tools should feel quiet, dense, organized, and built for repeated
  work. Avoid marketing-page composition.
- Games should hit Namleh's premium visual bar and may be more expressive.
- Do not put UI cards inside other cards.
- Do not hide primary workflows behind decorative hero content.
- Do not use visible in-app tutorial text to describe controls that should be
  self-evident.
- Text must fit on mobile and desktop.

## Storage

Use the narrowest durable place:

| Scope | Path |
| --- | --- |
| Current repo only | `.gstack-namleh/design-memory.md` |
| Shared Namleh gstack test repo | `.gstack-namleh/design-memory/<project>.md` |
| Final production plugin later | Namleh Codex plugin memory or Ops Platform design system |

Append entries. Never overwrite prior preference history unless the user says a
rule is obsolete.

## Entry Format

```markdown
## YYYY-MM-DD - <project/screen>

Signal: approved | rejected | caution
Context: <what was being reviewed>
Rule: <one reusable rule>
Evidence: <screenshot/file/link if available>
Applies to: <Ops Platform, client portals, games, all Namleh>
```

## Apply Flow

1. Read project `AGENTS.md` and existing design system files.
2. Read the relevant design-memory file if it exists.
3. Check the current UI against captured rules.
4. Fix concrete violations when the user asked for implementation.
5. Report only the changes and remaining design risks.
